In the valley below lived Cinder, a fox with soot on her whiskers from sniffing cold ashes. She saw grandmothers warming stones with their breath, children naming stars “little hearths,” and wolves lying beside lambs because all were equally frozen.

One night, beneath the ice, Cinder heard a patient ticking. A copper beetle crawled out, bearing a silver seed with a red heart. “Plant it where paths cross,” it said, “not behind walls, and the ember will wake.”

