But Cinder scratched the hard earth until her paws bled, then tucked the seed beneath the meeting of roads. The ground shuddered. A tree of living fire rose, scattering coal-fruit, bee-sparks, and moths bright as morning. Warmth entered every den, cradle, stable, and stranger’s cloak.

The stag lifted its antlers; the blue bowl cracked.

Since then, fire returns to hands that pass it on.
